      "writtenOn": "2026-04-14T17:27:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e It seems that it is still unlikely to significantly impact fio performance, since we are talking about short-living DMA mappings anyway. \n\nYeah. I was a trying to see how it performs with IO happening in parallel(njobs\u003d4 test) where flushing the cache while an io is happening for another thread.\n\n\u003e And even if it has significant impact on fio performance specifically in the scenario when fio is done in parallel with pVM boot, that doesn\u0027t seem a very important scenario to care about, since pVM boot per se is a rare event?\n\nAgreed.\n\n\u003e Anyway, looking at your numbers, with pVM coalesced flush appears \"mostly better\" than global flush in term of performance but \"mostly worse\" in terms of power, and without pVM it appears \"mostly worse\" in terms of both perf and power? So it doesn\u0027t seem convincing that it is a good idea?\n\nI thought the power numbers for pVM were good for coalesced case as the global flush was showing increased power usage(power diff% was mostly postive, nested_lazy was an exception). BW diff% are not so conclusive I guess. And Without pVM, looks like power diff was mostly positive and BW diff% was mostly negative.\n\nI shall remove this optimization in the next iteration",
